{% set local_problem %}
<p>Decatur sits in one of the most challenging drainage environments in North Alabama. The Tennessee River doesn't just pass by — it wraps around the city. Wheeler Lake sits to the east. The Wheeler Wildlife Refuge covers thousands of acres of wetland nearby. Water is everywhere, including underground.</p>

<p>That means Decatur's water table is naturally elevated. In low-lying areas — near Point Mallard, parts of Southwest Decatur, along the river — groundwater can rise to just a few feet below foundation level during wet periods. When TVA releases water through the dam system after regional rainfall, levels rise even higher.</p>

<p>Here's the problem: when your foundation is already dealing with groundwater pressure, you can't afford to add roof runoff to the mix. Standard downspout extensions that dump water 4-6 feet from your home aren't adequate. That water lands on clay soil that won't absorb it, pools against your foundation, and adds hydrostatic pressure to walls already under stress.</p>

<p>Underground drains change the equation entirely. Instead of depositing water a few feet from your foundation and hoping for the best, they route it 20, 30, or 50+ feet away through sealed pipes. The water never touches foundation soil. It exits at a discharge point far from your home — at the property edge, into a storm drain, or wherever it can safely disperse.</p>

<p>For Decatur homeowners dealing with wet basements, musty crawl spaces, foundation cracks, or chronic moisture issues, underground drains are often the only solution that actually works.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water through a sealed, sloped channel to a discharge point far from your home. They work continuously and automatically — every time it rains, water is routed away before it can threaten your foundation.</p>

<p>We use 4-inch corrugated or solid PVC pipe, depending on your specific conditions. The system is designed with proper slope (typically 1-2% grade) to ensure water flows away continuously rather than pooling inside the pipe. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits release water at the discharge point.</p>

<p>For Decatur installations, we pay special attention to flood conditions. If your property experiences standing water during TVA releases or heavy regional rainfall, we design systems that continue working even when surface conditions are wet. Discharge points are positioned to ensure water always has somewhere to go.</p>

<p>Unlike French drains (which collect groundwater already in the soil), underground gutter drains specifically handle roof runoff before it reaches the ground. They work with your gutter system to create complete water management — protecting your foundation from the one water source you can actually control.</p>
{% endset %}

